 # Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling)
 
-Also called: Importance Sampling from PDF, Inverse CDF Sampling, PDF Sampling, Stratified Sampling
+Also called: Inverse CDF Sampling
 
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) is a method for generating importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of a discrete probability distribution derived from per-bin weights [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of inefficient uniform sampling by concentrating samples in high-contribution regions (such as surfaces or dense volumes) while spreading them in empty or occluded areas [S1, S5, unofficial]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md), which treats all depth intervals equally, this method uses the probability density function (PDF) to guide sample placement, reducing variance in the final image [S3, S5, unofficial]. It serves as the core sampling mechanism in hierarchical rendering pipelines, where coarse samples inform the weights for fine sampling [S1, S3, unofficial].
+Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) generates importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of a discrete probability distribution derived from per-bin weights [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of inefficient uniform sampling by concentrating samples in high-contribution regions while spreading them in empty or occluded areas [S1, S5, unofficial]. Unlike [Uniform Ray Sampling](1261-uniform-ray-sampling.md), which treats all depth intervals equally, this method uses the probability density function (PDF) to guide sample placement, reducing variance in the final image [S3, S5, unofficial]. It serves as the core sampling mechanism in hierarchical rendering pipelines, where coarse samples inform the weights for fine sampling [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
 *   **Inputs:**
-    *   `weights`: A 2D tensor of unnormalized weights per bin, shape `(batch_size, num_bins)` [S1, unofficial]. These weights typically represent volume density or opacity estimates from a coarse pass [S1, S5, unofficial].
+    *   `weights`: A 2D tensor of unnormalized weights per bin, shape `(batch_size, num_bins)` [S1, unofficial].
     *   `bins`: A 1D tensor of bin boundaries along the ray, shape `(num_bins + 1,)` [S1, unofficial]. These define the spatial intervals $[z_i, z_{i+1}]$ [S1, unofficial].
     *   `N_samples`: Integer specifying the number of samples to draw per ray [S1, unofficial].
